# .secret-scan-allow — documented false positives for
#   node scripts/scan-secrets.js --dir . --fail-on=high
#
# Format: `<pattern> | <reason>`; `#` starts a comment.
#
#   <pattern>                      legacy — matches (case-insensitive substring)
#                                  the finding's file path OR its rule label.
#                                  Only vendored documentation SUBTREES may use
#                                  this form, and only with a trailing `/`
#   file:<sub> + label:<sub>       NARROW — the finding must match both, so one
#                                  rule in one file is documented without
#                                  silencing that file's other rules. A `label:`
#                                  term is a LITERAL case-insensitive substring
#                                  of the rule label — not a regex — so write
#                                  `label:exec() call`, never `label:exec\(\)`
#
# 60 entries. Read by BOTH `scripts/scan-secrets.js` (CI) and the in-loop
# `evidenceSecurity` gate through the shared `security-scanner.js` helpers, so
# the two can never disagree about what is allowed.
#
# An entry WITHOUT a reason is a hard error (exit 2) — a false positive that
# nobody documented is a rule that quietly rots.
#
# Policy: never blanket-allow a first-party source directory, and never allow an
# EXECUTABLE tree by directory — an allowed directory is an invisible one, and a
# real secret dropped into `scripts/` would pass. Executable trees below are
# allowed file-by-file and rule-by-rule. Vendored documentation subtrees
# (Markdown/reference material whose job is to show API-key examples) are the
# only directories allowed as directories, and each carries that reason inline.
#
# A first-party FILE entry must carry a `label:`. An entry that names a file and
# no label silences every rule in that file, so its stated reason is narrower
# than the thing it actually allows — `scripts/test-security-scanner.js` was
# allowed for its synthetic regex-`exec` fixtures while silently passing a
# planted `ghp_`. `scripts/test-security-scanner.js` asserts both halves of that
# rule against this file, so it cannot drift back.
#
# `parseAllowPattern` splits a pattern on `+`, so a `file:` value must not
# contain one. No path in this repo does.

