# --- first-party: the scanner scanning itself --------------------------------
# The binary guard used to embed a raw NUL byte, which made the scanner treat
# its OWN source as binary and skip it. Fixed, it now scans itself and matches
# the literals of the rules it defines. Allowed per rule, never per file, so a
# planted GitHub token in either copy is still Critical. The three terms below
# are the PARENTHESISED tails of the call-site rule labels — anchoring on them
# keeps the critical `Raw SQL string concatenation` and `Path traversal` labels
# blocking instead of matching a bare `risk)` substring.
file:bundle/hooks/lib/security-scanner.js + label:private key | the rule source holds the RSA/SSH header literals its own rules search for
file:bundle/hooks/lib/security-scanner.js + label:(code injection risk) | the eval() / new Function() rule labels end in the same call-site text their rules search for
file:bundle/hooks/lib/security-scanner.js + label:(XSS risk) | the innerHTML / document.write() rule labels end in the same call-site text their rules search for
file:bundle/hooks/lib/security-scanner.js + label:(command injection risk) | the exec() / spawn() / child_process rule labels end in the same call-site text their rules search for
file:bundle/opencode/hooks/lib/security-scanner.js + label:private key | byte-identical mirror of bundle/hooks/lib/security-scanner.js
file:bundle/opencode/hooks/lib/security-scanner.js + label:(code injection risk) | byte-identical mirror of bundle/hooks/lib/security-scanner.js
